# Are people as irrational as the experiments say?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "When do markets and experience correct biases? What did Gigerenzer argue about smart heuristics?"
next: {"title":"Do people care about fairness?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/91-do-people-care-about-fairness.cast"}

## Dealers don't cling
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Frequent traders
      - Dealers
      - Rare traders
    values:
      - 47
      - 45
      - 7
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - blue
      - blue
      - red
    reference:
      value: 50
      label: no endowment effect
      color: grey
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 65
    y_label: Swapped (%)
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 130
      'y': 95
      w: 760
      h: 360
    x_label: Orlando card show, 1999
sources:
    - id: knetsch1989
      title: The endowment effect and evidence of nonreversible indifference curves
      authors: Jack L. Knetsch
      year: 1989
      finding: 89 percent of those endowed with a mug kept it rather than trade for a chocolate bar; 90 percent of those endowed with the chocolate kept it (American Economic Review 79(5)).
    - id: list2003
      title: Does market experience eliminate market anomalies?
      authors: John A. List
      year: 2003
      finding: Sportscard show, Orlando, December 1999; Cal Ripken ticket stub vs Nolan Ryan certificate. Inexperienced nondealers (<6 trades a month) traded 6.8% (3 of 44); experienced nondealers 46.7% (14 of 30); dealers 45.7% and 43.6%. In a follow-up a year later, 13 of 21 who had increased their trading swapped. Quarterly Journal of Economics 118(1), 41-71.
    - id: list2011
      title: Does market experience eliminate market anomalies? The case of exogenous market experience
      authors: John A. List
      year: 2011
      finding: 'A framed field experiment that assigns market experience at random: market experience alone can eliminate the WTA-WTP gap (American Economic Review 101(3), 313-17).'

    box cardA "A: Biases are real\nKahneman, Tversky" #b5482e x 270 y 600 width 400 height 100 radius 14 font_size 30 hidden true
    box cardB "B: Smart in the wild\nGigerenzer, List" #2f6b8f x 730 y 600 width 400 height 100 radius 14 font_size 30 hidden true
    text everyw "follow us everywhere" #b5482e x 270 y 532 font_size 26 hidden true
    mark strikeA around everyw kind strike hidden true
    text fades "✓ fades with practice" #2f6b8f x 730 y 532 font_size 28 hidden true
    icon mug size 110 set twemoji x 330 y 330 icon_look picture of "hot beverage" hidden true
    text mugcue "Mug swap:\n9 in 10 kept" cites ["knetsch1989"] x 560 y 330 font_size 40 hidden true
    text rand "2011, at random: gap closes" cites ["list2011"] x 765 y 322 font_size 24 hidden true
    icon ticket size 110 set twemoji x 380 y 318 or ["ticket"] icon_look picture of "admission tickets" hidden true
    icon scroll size 110 set twemoji x 620 y 318 or ["page facing up"] icon_look picture of scroll hidden true
    text n344 "3 of 44" #b5482e cites ["list2003"] x 765 y 305 font_size 26 hidden true
    text year "a year later: trade more → swap" cites ["list2003"] x 765 y 362 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Are people as irrational as the experiments say?"

Are people as irrational as the experiments say? I'll argue yes: the biases are real, a (@draw everyw@)nd they follow us everywhere.
    draw cardA

B: And I'll argue the lab overstates them. Give people practice and a market, and many biases shrink.
    draw cardB

    pause 0.3

Start with the endowment effect: once a thing is yours, it seems worth more. In the classic mug swap, nine (@draw mugcue@) in ten kept what they were handed.
    draw mug

B: Students, in a classroom, with a mug they never wanted. In Decembe (@draw axes@)r 1999 John List ran the same swap on the floor of a sportscard show in Orlando.
    erase mug mugcue

Half the visitors got a (@draw ticket@) Cal Ripken ticket stu (@draw scroll@)b, half a Nolan Ryan certificate, worth about the same. With no endo (@draw reference_1@)wment effect, about half should swap.

B: These are collectors. They know what things are worth. I'd say close to half of them swapped.
    highlight reference_1

    erase ticket scroll

    quiz "What share of collectors who rarely trade swapped?" judge false
        right: Here is what List found.
        * About half
        * About a quarter
        * Under one in ten

Under one in ten. Three of the forty-four collecto (@draw n344@)rs who trade less than six times a month gave up what they had been handed.
    draw bar_3

    erase n344

B: Now those who trade six or more times a month: forty-seven percent swapped.
    draw bar_1

B: And the professional dealers: about forty-five. Close to the fifty that no endowment effect predicts.
    draw bar_2

    pause 0.3

Fair. But maybe people who never felt the pull are just the ones who became traders.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

B: List checked. A year later (@draw year@), collectors who had started trading more were more likely to swap. And in 2011 he hand (@draw rand@)ed out market experience at random, and the gap closed again.

    erase rand year

Then a point to you: the endowment effect fades where people trade often.
    draw strikeA fades

    pause 0.5

## Linda, counted
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Probability wording
      - Frequency wording
    values:
      - 87
      - 17
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - red
      - blue
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: 'Linda: wrong (%)'
    x_label: medians across studies
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 110
      w: 520
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: tk1983
      title: 'Extensional versus intuitive reasoning: the conjunction fallacy in probability judgment'
      authors: Amos Tversky, Daniel Kahneman
      year: 1983
      finding: Most people rank 'bank teller and active in the feminist movement' above 'bank teller'; the paper also reports that frequency formats markedly reduce conjunction errors (health-survey problem).
    - id: hg1999
      title: 'The ''conjunction fallacy'' revisited: how intelligent inferences look like reasoning errors'
      authors: Ralph Hertwig, Gerd Gigerenzer
      year: 1999
      finding: 'Median conjunction violations on the Linda problem: 87% with probability ranking (17 conditions in 10 studies, Hertwig and Chase 1998) vs 17% with frequency estimation (5 studies); ''probability'' is polysemous. Journal of Behavioral Decision Making 12, 275-305.'
    - id: kt1996
      title: On the reality of cognitive illusions
      authors: Daniel Kahneman, Amos Tversky
      year: 1996
      finding: With frequency formats in a between-subjects design (each group judging only one of the statements), the mean estimate for 'bank tellers and active feminists' exceeded that for 'bank tellers'. Psychological Review 103, 582-591.
    - id: gg2002
      title: 'Models of ecological rationality: the recognition heuristic'
      authors: Daniel G. Goldstein, Gerd Gigerenzer
      year: 2002
      finding: Asked whether San Diego or San Antonio has more inhabitants, about two thirds of American students but 100% of German students answered correctly; about half the Germans had not heard of San Antonio. Psychological Review 109, 75-90.
    - id: census2020
      title: 2010 and 2020 Censuses, city populations
      authors: US Census Bureau
      year: 2021
      finding: 'April 1, 2010 counts: San Antonio 1,327,407; San Diego 1,307,402. April 1, 2020: San Antonio 1,434,625; San Diego 1,386,932.'

    box linda "Linda: 31,\nphilosophy, protests" x 820 y 520 width 320 height 110 radius 14 font_size 28 hidden true
    link linkl href https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/casts/linda-problem.cast title "Drawcast: the Linda problem" form text x 820 y 425 hidden true
    portrait gig x 830 y 470 width 190 of "Gerd Gigerenzer" cameo true hidden true
    text kt96 "1996: one statement\neach → error returns" #b5482e cites ["kt1996"] x 820 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text eco "ecological rationality" #2f6b8f x 500 y 580 font_size 44 hidden true
    box sd "San Diego" x 300 y 450 width 300 height 90 radius 14 font_size 36 hidden true
    box sa "San Antonio" x 700 y 450 width 300 height 90 radius 14 font_size 36 hidden true
    text vs "bigger?" #8f887c x 500 y 450 font_size 30 hidden true
    text score "Germans: 100% right\nAmericans: about 2/3" cites ["gg2002"] x 500 y 320 font_size 34 hidden true
    text census "2010 census: San Antonio bigger" #b5482e cites ["census2020"] x 500 y 220 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards sortc bins ["Fits its world","Misfires"] check each hidden true
        Fits its world: Bigger city
        * "Better painkiller, one heavily advertised" bin Misfires
        * "Safer airline, one in crash news" bin Misfires
    text moral "smart = rule that fits its world" #2f6b8f x 500 y 220 font_size 36 hidden true
    text opts "teller\nvs\nfeminist teller" x 820 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon key size 110 set twemoji x 400 y 400 icon_look picture of key hidden true
    icon lock size 110 set twemoji x 600 y 400 or ["lock"] icon_look picture of locked hidden true
    text klabel "rule ↔ world: must fit" #8f887c x 500 y 310 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c200 "of 200 women:\nhow many?" #2f6b8f cites ["hg1999"] x 830 y 225 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Linda, counted"

Dealers learn. My side's best case has nothing to do with marke (@draw linda@)ts, though. Meet Linda: thirty-one, a philosophy graduate, active in protests.

(@draw axes@) Is she more likely a bank teller, or a bank teller activ (@draw opts@)e in the feminist movement? Most people pick the second, yet it can never be more likely than the first.

Tversky and Kahneman called it the conjunction fallacy.  (@draw linkl@)A whole drawcast on drawcast.app, the Linda problem, walks  (@draw bar_1@)through it. Across studies, a median of about 87 percent get it wrong.

    erase linda linkl opts

B: Gerd Gigerenzer's reply: in everyday speech, probable means plausible, fitting the story. People aren't failing at logic; they're answering a different question.
    draw gig

B: Ask it in counts  (@draw c200@)instead. Of two hundred women like Linda, how many are bank tellers, and how many are feminist bank tellers? T (@draw bar_2@)he median error falls to about 17 percent.

    erase gig c200

Kahneman and Tversky had reported that counts help back in 1983. But in 1996 they showed that when eac (@draw kt96@)h person sees only one of the two statements, the error comes back, even in counts.

    erase kt96 axes bar_1 bar_2

B: Gigerenzer's bigger claim is that s (@draw eco@)hortcuts aren't bugs. A simple rule is sma (@draw key lock klabel parallel true@)rt when it fits its world. He called it ecological rationality.

    erase key lock klabel

B: Which city is bigger, San Diego or San Antonio? Every German stude (@draw score@)nt asked got it right, against about two thirds of the Americans.
    draw sd vs sa

B: Many Germans had never heard of San Antonio, so they picked the one they knew. In their world, fame tracked size.
    highlight sd effect circle

Change the world and the same rule misfire (@draw census@)s. Since the 2010 census, San Antonio has been the bigger city.

    erase score census eco sd sa vs

    draw sortc

    ask "Sort each case for the rule 'pick the one you recognise'." on sortc store f stage own
        intro: Your turn: where does 'pick the one you recognise' fit its world, and where does it misfire?
        right: The cities fit; the other two misfire, because ads and crashes make a name famous, not good.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try: the cities fit; the painkiller and the airline misfire.

(@draw sd vs sa parallel true@) So a shortcut is neither smart nor dumb by itself. (@draw moral@) It depends on the world it meets.

    pause 0.5

## Who keeps the point?
sources:
    - id: gm2001
      title: 'Loss aversion and seller behavior: evidence from the housing market'
      authors: David Genesove, Christopher Mayer
      year: 2001
      finding: 'Downtown Boston condominiums, 1990-97: owners facing nominal losses set higher asking prices (25-35% of the expected loss) and sold at a much lower rate; the effect for investors is about half that for owner-occupants. Quarterly Journal of Economics 116(4), 1233-1260.'
    - id: mhk2001
      title: Do frequency representations eliminate conjunction effects? An exercise in adversarial collaboration
      authors: Barbara Mellers, Ralph Hertwig, Daniel Kahneman
      year: 2001
      finding: Kahneman and Hertwig agreed on joint tests of the Linda problem with Mellers as arbiter; frequency formats alone did not eliminate conjunction effects, and the authors 'did not agree when they began the collaboration, nor do they agree now'. Psychological Science 12(4), 269-275.
    - id: list2003
      title: Does market experience eliminate market anomalies?
      authors: John A. List
      year: 2003
      finding: Dealers and experienced collectors show little endowment effect; inexperienced collectors a large one.
    - id: hg1999
      title: The 'conjunction fallacy' revisited
      authors: Ralph Hertwig, Gerd Gigerenzer
      year: 1999
      finding: Frequency wording cuts conjunction errors on the Linda problem (medians 87% to 17%).

    box hA "A: Biases are real" #b5482e x 270 y 620 width 400 height 70 radius 14 font_size 32 hidden true
    box hB "B: Smart in the wild" #2f6b8f x 730 y 620 width 400 height 70 radius 14 font_size 32 hidden true
    text b1 "✓ dealers swap" #2f6b8f cites ["list2003"] x 730 y 545 font_size 32 hidden true
    text b2 "✓ shortcuts fit their world" #2f6b8f cites ["hg1999"] x 730 y 495 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon house size 80 set twemoji x 430 y 360 icon_look picture of house hidden true
    text a1 "✓ Boston condos:\nlosers ask more" #b5482e cites ["gm2001"] x 270 y 525 font_size 32 hidden true
    text b3 "✓ investors: half the effect" #2f6b8f cites ["gm2001"] x 730 y 445 font_size 32 hidden true
    text a2 "✓ sellers can profit\nfrom a bias" #b5482e x 270 y 430 font_size 32 hidden true
    text split "practice + feedback → fade   ·   once in a life → stay" x 500 y 335 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st1 "Shrink with practice: MOSTLY AGREED" x 500 y 262 font_size 36 hidden true
    text st2 "Matter for big, rare choices: CONTESTED" #b5482e x 500 y 204 font_size 36 hidden true
    text collab "Kahneman + Hertwig, 2001:\none study, still disagree" #8a5fa8 cites ["mhk2001"] x 500 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon pension size 80 set twemoji x 570 y 360 icon_look picture of "money bag" hidden true

    card title "Who keeps the point?"

    draw hA hB parallel true

B: Deal (@draw b1@)ers learn, and good shortc (@draw b2@)uts fit their world. So the lab exaggerates?

Not so fast. Dealers trade every week and see what a mistake costs. The big choices come once o (@draw house@)r twice in a life: a hom (@draw pension@)e, a pension.

    erase house pension

In Boston in the 1990s, condo owners who fac (@draw a1@)ed selling at a loss asked noticeably more, and sold more slowly. That's loss aversion in a real market.

B: And the investors, who do thi (@draw b3@)s for a living, showed about half the effect. Experience shrinks it.

Half is not none. And a market doesn't always punish a bias. (@draw a2@) A seller can profit from it instead, like a gym contract sold to the self we plan to be.

B: Then the line r (@draw split@)uns here: with practice and quick feedback, biases fade. Once in a life, they can stay.

How sure are we? That b (@draw st1@)iases shrink with practice and feedback: mostly agreed.

B: How mu (@draw st2@)ch they matter for life's big, rare choices: contested.

    erase split

B: In 2001 Kahneman and Ralph Hertwig, a Gigerenze (@draw collab@)r co-author, even ran a joint Linda study to settle it. They finished still disagreeing about what it meant.

    pause 0.4

    erase collab

    erase st1 st2

    quiz "At a weekend flea market, who is likelier to price her own old lamp above what any buyer will pay?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The first-timer: like the collectors who rarely traded, she has no practice at letting go.
        wrong: Think of the collectors who rarely traded: here, the first-timer.
        * A stallholder who sells there every week
        + A first-timer clearing out her attic
        * Both equally

    quiz "A doctor misreads what a positive result on a '90 percent accurate' test means. On Gigerenzer's view, what helps most?" voice b
        right: Counts: the same facts in the format our minds handle well, as with Linda.
        wrong: Remember what happened to Linda's errors: here, counts.
        + Say it in counts: of 1,000 people tested, how many test positive and are ill
        * Warn the doctor about bias
        * Order more tests

    quiz "Where would you expect a bias to survive longest?"
        right: The pension: one try, and the feedback arrives forty years later.
        wrong: Where is there least practice and slowest feedback? Here, the pension.
        * Pricing cards at a weekly show
        + Choosing a pension plan once, at 25
        * A professional poker player's bets

    draw st1 st2

So are people as irrational as the experiments say? Where they practise, often less. Where they choose once, perhaps just as much.
    highlight st1 st2

B: Which means how people choose depends on where they are choosing, and how often they get to try.
    highlight hA hB

    pause 0.5

